comments.body:
PR review: The PR body names the stage-3 and stage-4 shuffle "vshufps", but the code uses `_mm512_shuffle_f32x4`, which is VSHUFF32X4, a different instruction with a different selector meaning [nit].

Evidence, lines 178-181:
```
__m512 p = _mm512_shuffle_f32x4(u[0][k], u[1][k], pair_sel);
__m512 q2 = _mm512_shuffle_f32x4(u[2][k], u[3][k], pair_sel);
...
    s_rows + k * s_stride_floats, _mm512_shuffle_f32x4(p, q2, quad_sel));
```
VSHUFF32X4 moves whole 128-bit lanes. VSHUFPS (`_mm512_shuffle_ps`) moves 32-bit elements inside each 128-bit lane. Under VSHUFPS the same immediates 0x88 and 0xdd select even and odd 32-bit elements inside each lane, not even and odd 128-bit lanes. A reviewer who follows question 1 with the vshufps table cannot reproduce the "lane `l` becomes row `4*l + k`" argument, which needs whole lanes to move. The code comment at lines 154-157 already names `_mm512_shuffle_f32x4`, so only the PR body needs the change.

Proposed fix in the PR body (section "How to review this PR", question 1, first bullet): replace "the `vshufps` selector" with "the `_mm512_shuffle_f32x4` (VSHUFF32X4, 128-bit lane shuffle) selector".

PR review: The Verification table says the kill-switch run skipped "2 sections", but the skip happens at the test-case level, so 2 test cases (holding 4 Catch2 sections) are skipped [nit].

Evidence, lines 316-318 (the same pattern repeats at lines 330-333 for the PV check):
```
if (!tron::amx_attn_h128g4::available()) {
  WARN("AMX unavailable on this host - QK precision check skipped");
  return;
```
The return leaves the test case before Catch2 enters either of its two sections ("4 heads: ..." at line 320 and "16 heads: ..." at line 323, and the pair at lines 334 and 337).

Reading cost: a reader who counts sections in the file (two per case) cannot match the number 2.

Proposed replacement for the table cell (row "AMX off (kill switch)"): "2 test cases (the QK and PV precision checks) skipped with 'AMX unavailable on this host'". Keep "4 pass" unchanged. Catch2 WARN leaves both skipped cases in the passed count.

ben-review: The unpacked score tile is described by two different expressions, `__m512 u[4][4]` at its definition and `__m512 (&u)[TILE_ROWS_16 / ROWS_PER_LANE_4][ROWS_PER_LANE_4]` at the new helper's parameter, so one local alias would define that shape once [nit].

Evidence (HEAD):
```
171     const __m512 (&u)[TILE_ROWS_16 / ROWS_PER_LANE_4][ROWS_PER_LANE_4],
233     __m512 u[4][4];
255     store_transposed_rows<0>(u, s_block, s_stride_floats);
```
The helper and its parameter type are new in this PR. Line 233 is pre-existing.

The concept is the 16 x 16 score tile after the two unpack stages, held as 4 row groups x 4 registers. Reading cost: at line 255 the reader must evaluate `TILE_ROWS_16 / ROWS_PER_LANE_4` to 4 to see that the reference binds to `u`. The compiler enforces the match, so this is a reading cost, not a defect.

Proposed fix, in the anonymous namespace next to the helper:
```cpp
// The 16 x 16 score tile after the two unpack stages: 4 row groups x 4
// registers, one register per output row of the row group.
using unpacked_tile = __m512[TILE_ROWS_16 / ROWS_PER_LANE_4][ROWS_PER_LANE_4];

template <int lane>
inline void store_transposed_rows(
    const unpacked_tile& u, float* s_block, size_t s_stride_floats) {
```
and at line 233 `unpacked_tile u;`. The `< 4` loop bounds at lines 231 and 236 are untouched by the diff and can stay.

ben-review: The lanes to store are chosen by testing one group size by name instead of deriving the lane count from `group_heads`, so a third accepted group size would leave rows 4..group_heads-1 unwritten with no compile-time signal [nit].

Evidence (HEAD lines 255-260):
```
    store_transposed_rows<0>(u, s_block, s_stride_floats);
    if constexpr (group_heads == GROUP_HEADS_16) {
      store_transposed_rows<1>(u, s_block, s_stride_floats);
      store_transposed_rows<2>(u, s_block, s_stride_floats);
      store_transposed_rows<3>(u, s_block, s_stride_floats);
    }
```
The current code is correct under the `requires(group_heads_ok(group_heads))` clause (only 4 and 16 are accepted). The quantity the code means is `group_heads / ROWS_PER_LANE_4` lanes, and ROWS_PER_LANE_4 (line 167) exists for exactly that relation.

Proposed replacement, with the two constants at the top of `qk_rowmajor_128x4` before the `for` loop at line 231. It keeps the emitted code of both instantiations (lane 0 only for 4 heads, lanes 0..3 for 16 heads):
```cpp
  static_assert(group_heads % ROWS_PER_LANE_4 == 0, "a stored lane is 4 rows");
  constexpr size_t lanes = group_heads / ROWS_PER_LANE_4;
  ...
    store_transposed_rows<0>(u, s_block, s_stride_floats);
    if constexpr (lanes > 1) store_transposed_rows<1>(u, s_block, s_stride_floats);
    if constexpr (lanes > 2) store_transposed_rows<2>(u, s_block, s_stride_floats);
    if constexpr (lanes > 3) store_transposed_rows<3>(u, s_block, s_stride_floats);
```
The comment at 227-230 ("only when group_heads is 16") and the PR body sentence "Lanes 1 to 3 exist only in the 16-head instantiation" would need the matching edit. This overlaps issue #5021 ("a future shape is a minimal code change"), so deferring it there is fine.

Wade-code-review: `check_qk` (:165-217, about 53 lines) and `check_pv` (:219-302, about 84 lines) are new helpers with no comment at their declaration [nit]. HEAD :165-166 read `template <size_t group_heads>` / `void check_qk() {`, with a blank line above. The third helper, `check_q_pack` at :132-133, carries its contract comment at :127-131. The sentinel comment at :158-162 explains the untouched-rows assertion of `check_qk` but not the dotter comparison, and the file header explains both kernels only in general terms. A reader of the helpers alone learns the comparison from the TEST_CASE names at :315 and :329, about 150 lines below. One two-line comment each is enough (group_heads = query heads per KV head). Above :165:

```
// QK kernel against the AVX dotter for group_heads query rows. Rows
// group_heads..15 of s must keep the sentinel.
```

Above :219:

```
// PV kernel against the RNE-weight reference and the truncating (AVX)
// weight reference for group_heads rows.
```

:220 is the same item, not a second nit.

Wade-code-review: the QK store branch keys on `group_heads == GROUP_HEADS_16` and treats every other size as 4 heads, and nothing enforces that at compile time [nit]. HEAD :255-256 read `store_transposed_rows<0>(u, s_block, s_stride_floats);` / `if constexpr (group_heads == GROUP_HEADS_16) {`. The comment at :227-230 names the assumption in prose ("lanes 1..3 (rows 4..15) only when group_heads is 16"), and the `requires(group_heads_ok(group_heads))` clause at :192 admits only 4 and 16 today, so a bug-free caller cannot reach the implicit else with anything but 4 (group_heads = query heads per KV head). The other two kernels are generic on the parameter (the pack loop at :138, the P loop at :285). If a third size is ever added to `group_heads_ok`, pack and PV would serve it, and this kernel would write rows 0..3 of `s` only, with no report. Question, not blocking: should the branch state its assumption so a third size fails to compile here? One form:

```
} else {
  static_assert(group_heads == GROUP_HEADS_4, "lane 0 covers the 4-head case only");
}
```

`lane` must stay a template argument (the shuffle selectors are immediates, :160-163), so a runtime lane loop is not an alternative.

cpp-coding-guide: The rewritten query-fill line names the new 0.0625 step but keeps 1.0f, `% 4` and 0.25f bare. [nit]

```
constexpr float HEAD_OFFSET_STEP_0_0625 = 0.0625f;
expected[i] = 1.0f + static_cast<float>(i / head_size) * HEAD_OFFSET_STEP_0_0625 +
              static_cast<float>(i % 4) * 0.25f;
```
(lines 145-147)

At BASE the line read `expected[i] = 1.0f + static_cast<float>(i % 4) * 0.25f;`, so the three literals predate the PR. The comment at lines 142-144 explains the new term only. One statement now mixes a named step with three bare values, and that reads as two different rules.

Proposed replacement for lines 145-147:
```
constexpr float QUERY_BASE_1_0 = 1.0f;
constexpr float HEAD_OFFSET_STEP_0_0625 = 0.0625f;
constexpr size_t VALUE_PERIOD_4 = 4;
constexpr float VALUE_STEP_0_25 = 0.25f;
expected[i] = QUERY_BASE_1_0 +
              static_cast<float>(i / head_size) * HEAD_OFFSET_STEP_0_0625 +
              static_cast<float>(i % VALUE_PERIOD_4) * VALUE_STEP_0_25;
```
The second-round fill at line 181 is unchanged and outside the diff.

positron-code-review: The file header ends in change narration ("and are kept") instead of the lasting reason the names mismatch the served shapes. [nit]

Rule: explain the current design, contract or failure mode instead of the process that produced the code. This is not about the names themselves (issue #5021 owns the rename). It is about the wording.

Evidence (lines 14-16):
```
// 128 with kv_mul 4 or 16 (group_heads_ok). The namespace name
// amx_attn_h128g4 and the _128x4 suffix of every kernel record the first
// shape, kv_mul 4, and are kept.
```

Reading cost: "kept" tells the reader the mismatch is deliberate, which is useful. It also implies a change event that a reader at a later commit cannot see, and the sentence does not say where the mismatch is resolved. (kv_mul is the number of query heads per KV head.)

Proposed fix, lines 14-15 unchanged, line 16 replaced by two sentences:
```suggestion
// shape, kv_mul 4. Issue #5021 tracks the rename.
```

simplify-cpp-comments: The 13-line comment above `LANES_EVEN_0X88` restates the selector names and the caller, and the bit-0 / bit-1 facts sit 11 lines away from the two selectors they explain [should-fix].

Evidence, HEAD lines 150-162, for example line 157 `// lanes (selector 0x88) or the odd lanes (selector 0xdd) of its two inputs.` and lines 161-162 `// ... Lane 0 = (0x88, 0x88) is the one store sequence the 4-head kernel emits.`

Rule 4 (every comment line maps to a code line) and Rule 2 (no restated identifiers). Lines 150-152 describe the caller's unpack loop (236-253), not this function. Lines 156-157 repeat the names of the two constants. Lines 161-162 are caller context already at line 229. Lines 158-160 belong next to `pair_sel` and `quad_sel` (174-175). Lines 152-153 repeat the ROWS_PER_LANE_4 comment at 165-166, so the fact should appear once.

Reading cost: 13 lines for two constants, with the selector rule in the wrong place.

Proposed, 5 lines above the constants and one line at each selector (all under the 88-column limit of .clang-format):

```
// Stages 3 and 4 of the 16 x 16 transpose in qk_rowmajor_128x4: output row
// 4 * lane + k (one query head, 16 tokens) is assembled from 128-bit lane
// `lane` of u[0..3][k], which holds tile column 4 * lane + k for tokens
// 4 * row_group .. 4 * row_group + 3. The shuffle selectors are immediates,
// so `lane` is a template argument.
constexpr int LANES_EVEN_0X88 = 0x88;
constexpr int LANES_ODD_0XDD = 0xdd;
```

```
  // Stage 3 pairs row groups 0/1 and 2/3: lanes {0, 2} or {1, 3} by bit 0.
  constexpr int pair_sel = (lane & 1) ? LANES_ODD_0XDD : LANES_EVEN_0X88;
  // Stage 4 joins the two pairs: the first or the second lane by bit 1.
  constexpr int quad_sel = (lane & 2) ? LANES_ODD_0XDD : LANES_EVEN_0X88;
```

simplify-cpp-comments: The transpose comment restates the `if constexpr` block it sits above [nit].

Evidence, HEAD lines 229-230:
```
  // store helper emits stages 3 and 4 for lane 0 (rows 0..3) always and for
  // lanes 1..3 (rows 4..15) only when group_heads is 16.
```

Rule 2 (do not restate). These two lines describe lines 255-260 directly: `store_transposed_rows<0>` is unconditional, and `<1>`, `<2>`, `<3>` sit under `if constexpr (group_heads == GROUP_HEADS_16)`. The row ranges "(rows 0..3)" and "(rows 4..15)" restate the helper's own comment and its lane offset at line 176. The facts the code does not say are the stage numbering and that the unpack loop runs for the whole tile at either group size.

Reading cost: the reader reads the branch twice, once as prose and once as code.

Proposed text for lines 227-230:
```
  // Transpose s_transposed(64x16) -> s rows 0..group_heads-1. Stages 1 and 2
  // (the unpack loop below) run for the whole tile at either group size; stages
